#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "3c7dd3ad-eaa9-5312-adaf-3a0772e845f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-762" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty { {{n}}(x-1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=1$ of $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty { {{n}}(x-1)^{n}} = (x-1) + {2(x-1)^2} + {3(x-1)^3} +\\cdots .\n$$\n\nTo find $f'(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+0+4(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+4(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the a General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "cb7a106a-130a-5479-b60d-8ea76a8cd8de" :question/math-academy-id "e-763" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{ (x-3)^{n}}{n}\n$$\n\nFind $a_n,$ given that\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/assets/7316ce9632bc2ab0347accb71c4c0dc90db8a87e0f9bead704e0218523a9b367.png)\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042403/assets/846a66292603d3436219826be2e9419b5798fa121341d1e1676fba63c0f37eec.png)\nWe can now write the Taylor series for $f'(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\na_n = (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "e656ee8d-55b9-5ea6-beae-7c2caf329a6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-764" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$  about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-3)^{n}\\,x^n}{n^3}\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x| <            R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of $f'(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of $f'(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3}}{(-3)^{n}\\,{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)x\\cdot {n}^{3}}{(n+1)^{3}}| \\\\  & =3|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3}} \\\\  & =3|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R = \\dfrac 1 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b1ff20f-c375-52a8-ac88-0679a78f9485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{4}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{3}{7}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316151} {:answer/id #uuid "7e51aaa7-d3fc-531e-aaea-4b31028cc64c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{6}{7}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316152} {:answer/id #uuid "8e69d0fb-cfce-5f75-8cc1-b3cacce4e286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{3}(x-1)+\\frac{6}{5}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316153} {:answer/id #uuid "47dd878e-b75e-5088-a612-e742524e5c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{4}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{16}{81}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316154} {:answer/id #uuid "da492dc5-edd3-5d82-a008-45a6ad5e601b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{16}{27}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e51aaa7-d3fc-531e-aaea-4b31028cc64c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{6}{7}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316152} :answer-field/id #uuid "498bacbd-078c-59c4-826e-caf996bc2d85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b94c18d-8d1b-52c1-b39b-7b21a15a8977" :question/math-academy-id "q-126101" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{5n-1}(x-1)^{n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=1$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{5n-1}(x-1)^{n}=(x-1)+\\frac{4}{9}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{7}(x-1)^{3}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1+\\frac{8}{9}(x-1)+\\frac{6}{7}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abd8e53d-ad49-505c-9a7f-4caa912fafa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316158} {:answer/id #uuid "59b3eaf0-8d1c-5156-a164-d2bd746c86f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316159} {:answer/id #uuid "c2df9d36-2e4d-54f3-8549-ab2e239fe8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316160} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1513bf-9e46-5c8d-b61a-97a5c856e212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316161} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf86e81-d76c-54ee-8587-12564a2b8205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abd8e53d-ad49-505c-9a7f-4caa912fafa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316158} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d9cb37-fb69-5132-bb14-d115532077ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "153b6d05-924f-5553-b86b-f60ba5f8d6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126166" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{2}^{2n}(n!)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2n+2}}{{2}^{2n+2}((n+1)!)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{2n}(n!)^{2}}{{x}^{2n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}(n+1)^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ Since $0<1$ for all $x,$ we conclude that $R=\\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5fe0b1b-6daa-504f-8b6e-5832932e8ce5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316165} {:answer/id #uuid "bc89ab29-05f0-5c86-a374-1266a83a8c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316166} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0693a7-f0d7-53a6-905e-bddd4a05b56d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316167} {:answer/id #uuid "02819df1-9f6b-5691-8459-3d7d9dfd8fbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316168} {:answer/id #uuid "802bd5c6-7d12-5a18-8504-c93612e2722c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d0693a7-f0d7-53a6-905e-bddd4a05b56d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316167} :answer-field/id #uuid "0691ab1c-aefd-59b7-a003-529f946303c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "621b9ccf-4fef-5845-a96d-7e9c92f431e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126168" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{2}^{n}n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n+1}(n+1)}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}n}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{xn}{2(n+1)}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{2} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3065f38-e681-5fc2-ae5a-1e1aeb081741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316172} {:answer/id #uuid "5a678b47-221a-5854-be61-31861d2700cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316173} {:answer/id #uuid "0a21cd9b-f189-54b7-be4b-0b575735e140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316174} {:answer/id #uuid "883b77bb-4aec-59e3-b2c4-e5ec5285707d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316175} {:answer/id #uuid "cef06e76-95d0-5e44-8808-4b3b56378ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "883b77bb-4aec-59e3-b2c4-e5ec5285707d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316175} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed3b3b4-e0cb-5bf2-a643-7ad5f17dc132" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "285f64d1-e80a-5824-a8d3-10c0a11a59c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126169" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\sqrt{n}\\,{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\sqrt{n+1}\\,{x}^{n+1}}{\\sqrt{n}\\,{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|x\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{n}}| \\\\  & =|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d54d31c-2f44-5004-9ef0-7af05064719d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d54d31c-2f44-5004-9ef0-7af05064719d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316179} :answer-field/id #uuid "f35eb089-80c0-5b33-aacf-f6037d2e88bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bba82dc8-c963-5310-b46f-07d48ffe771b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bba82dc8-c963-5310-b46f-07d48ffe771b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316181} :answer-field/id #uuid "99e9e259-8a89-57ad-a41d-bc92f3f83eb4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4dfbd8e-4627-5f94-9677-ee06de24d096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4dfbd8e-4627-5f94-9677-ee06de24d096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316183} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a80f65-3b29-530d-abdb-39038ef9fd1a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "994997bb-b8ce-54ce-b382-8fc1a9a242b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-347641" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}(x-2)^{n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=2$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-2)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-2)^{2}+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}(x-2)^{n}=\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)-\\frac{1}{6}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}(x-2)^{3}-⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)+\\frac{1}{8}(x-2)^{2}+⋯.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053ddb29-11d0-5a4f-940b-9dd02f0e393a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-2x+2x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316186} {:answer/id #uuid "a051163c-531a-50f9-bb07-42a4175962d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-2x+x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a051163c-531a-50f9-bb07-42a4175962d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-2x+x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316187} :answer-field/id #uuid "9899a966-2954-5709-b2ed-bbff61e60870" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "645278df-e8b4-5cb1-9804-1f40dac1d27f" :question/math-academy-id "q-352355" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{(n+1)!}{x}^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\,⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{(n+1)!}{x}^{n+1}=2x-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{4}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2-2x+{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016d6cbe-6890-5798-bc74-19b38b76e617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "016d6cbe-6890-5798-bc74-19b38b76e617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316190} :answer-field/id #uuid "d938233a-60f7-5538-90aa-b1de6862a2ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316189} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15580e9c-e89e-5bb2-9039-c9e38f72d799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15580e9c-e89e-5bb2-9039-c9e38f72d799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316192} :answer-field/id #uuid "9af74b40-6e38-56d7-87f6-bbcfffdfac2b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316191} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60bab7c4-abc4-5b00-9102-6f088b816286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60bab7c4-abc4-5b00-9102-6f088b816286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316194} :answer-field/id #uuid "760263e7-1a9a-50b5-be33-1390a75ae293" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7cc3f496-a737-5718-b113-9c4166db0722" :question/math-academy-id "q-352512" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{2}^{2n+1}}{(2n)!}(x+2)^{n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=-2$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,-\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x+2)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x+2)^{2}+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{2}^{2n+1}}{(2n)!}(x+2)^{n}=4(x+2)-\\frac{4}{3}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{8}{45}(x+2)^{3}-⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4-\\frac{8}{3}(x+2)+\\frac{8}{15}(x+2)^{2}+⋯.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the a General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a35b1334-b31c-5538-ae6a-e2b72290a957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(2n+1)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316197} {:answer/id #uuid "b57780e3-4b73-588e-873e-843c89d261dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b57780e3-4b73-588e-873e-843c89d261dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316198} :answer-field/id #uuid "f107d698-748e-5ae1-bf7c-72d754507b3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1290689-d3f8-5530-9014-775181dd0080" :question/math-academy-id "q-352520" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}\n$(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{120}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{(2n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2x}{120}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n{x}^{n-1}}{(2n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}}{(2n+1)!} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the a General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5c180e7-0c88-5792-a8f3-ce8a8992c799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5c180e7-0c88-5792-a8f3-ce8a8992c799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316201} :answer-field/id #uuid "e00af366-fbb1-5dd8-9938-35068adeed18" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8407692-ad71-5dcf-a84f-84768eae401d" :question/math-academy-id "q-352524" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯+{{\\frac{2\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x+2{x}^{2}+⋯+{{\\frac{2n(n+1){x}^{n}}{(n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(n+1){x}^{n}}{(n+1)!} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n!}{x}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9f51430-bf4b-5453-aa8f-598990fce78d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316204} {:answer/id #uuid "98f98b4d-c18e-532b-9aab-1d59adda8624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98f98b4d-c18e-532b-9aab-1d59adda8624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316205} :answer-field/id #uuid "03dad710-d1ab-5c72-b54c-b77aff68ccab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f7cf8d8-89e9-586b-bd0c-70903e69de5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-352603" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(4n-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$\n\nThe radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(4(n+1)-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n+1}}{(4n-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{4n+3}{4n-1}||\\frac{x}{4}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{4} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1426fe6-071e-5275-a65c-b401cc7a3555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316208} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c861d1-e977-5ec3-a050-f5e295912425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316209} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad0d17a-8b12-5c59-afe4-4c00dae74eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-x+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316210} {:answer/id #uuid "4553e274-fd97-56ad-ba12-467d7b438250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316211} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b85e41-7ed0-5f0b-9554-c831cbd34747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=x-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8b85e41-7ed0-5f0b-9554-c831cbd34747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=x-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316212} :answer-field/id #uuid "c700eca3-e127-530c-8751-d6bcb162dca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4496a6e7-30b7-546b-ab2d-8726aead40e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-50867" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n-1}\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n}}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n-1}\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=x-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-⋯.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the a General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a362a977-8ee1-54dd-b058-a6409bdb0c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{k}=k+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316215} {:answer/id #uuid "369b7bc9-4fd1-5869-a2a5-7049afcc2bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{k}=k-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316216} {:answer/id #uuid "7a78614d-dce2-5e47-b122-c11fbea6996c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{k}=2k-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316217} {:answer/id #uuid "b223640a-3ddd-5cfa-8431-64ea9dce826d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{k}=3k+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316218} {:answer/id #uuid "cef3c420-d58b-5e72-811a-2f3f70d08b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{k}=k+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cef3c420-d58b-5e72-811a-2f3f70d08b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{k}=k+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316219} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3a244f3-0e76-503d-a4c1-f7b9891e848f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c68fbae-dfe4-54d2-9aa1-df486827bf51" :question/math-academy-id "q-50870" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k+1}.\n$$\n\nFind ${a}_{k},$ given that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{k}(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2(x-1)^{2}+4(x-1)^{3}-⋯+{{(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k+1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }k\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-4(x-1)+12(x-1)^{2}-⋯+{{(k+1)(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }k\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }(k+1)(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}=k+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8cd5d00-ea3d-5fa8-a130-a67036d28479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316222} {:answer/id #uuid "4c13d601-2a23-5482-82e2-141ea6f14eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316223} {:answer/id #uuid "2f444865-d21f-5feb-b836-0920a4684f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316224} {:answer/id #uuid "e6021bdb-405b-51db-9d31-83ab2ccf1c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316225} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a1667f-0d81-57d8-aed7-5598ba51d8a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c13d601-2a23-5482-82e2-141ea6f14eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316223} :answer-field/id #uuid "0df45b32-6787-537b-9b98-e13e75cdf185" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5628210-fc1a-5299-9818-135e7bc965f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-50912" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{2}^{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{2}^{n+1}{x}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n}{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|2x| \\\\  & =2|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the a General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ba3f42-ef1b-55d4-a8de-f2817522b843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n+1}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316229} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1ed441-e039-5e2b-b6ea-1c24155cffd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316230} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bb65a7-4823-50ae-9395-6395e785e292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316231} {:answer/id #uuid "ba65f3eb-8d0e-5e9e-82ec-d089de1ec45e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+2)^{n+1}}{(n-1)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316232} {:answer/id #uuid "96579ecb-3c20-50bd-8547-ba64e244fd12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=(n+1)(x+2)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9bb65a7-4823-50ae-9395-6395e785e292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316231} :answer-field/id #uuid "efcb5a57-0958-55b8-ba11-dcc39fd3a1e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c635d17-f111-534f-904a-e9d7c13565f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83461" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}\\frac{(x+2)^{n+1}}{n!}.\n$$\n\nFind ${a}_{n},$ given that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}{a}_{n}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2!}(x+2)^{3}-⋯+{{(-1)^{n}\\frac{(x+2)^{n+1}}{n!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-2(x+2)+\\frac{3}{2!}(x+2)^{2}-⋯+{{(-1)^{n}\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the a General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "942a0268-23c3-5a7c-9023-790f33959eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316236} {:answer/id #uuid "bd766eb0-a8e9-5507-ba3b-84c83bfc66c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{n(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316237} {:answer/id #uuid "a720682d-1f1c-5112-93ac-4157d26e4979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316238} {:answer/id #uuid "33105749-7c89-500c-8acb-1ec946bd20de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n}}{n+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316239} {:answer/id #uuid "efe3d76d-abe1-50fc-9b91-7f9d4786a82f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{n(x-3)^{n}}{n+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a720682d-1f1c-5112-93ac-4157d26e4979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316238} :answer-field/id #uuid "62eb5535-47de-53a3-8cf0-a9b4150e811d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6988f0ba-d85e-54f8-8617-cb0143aad708" :question/math-academy-id "q-83463" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{n(x-3)^{n}}{n+3}.\n$$\n\nFind ${a}_{n},$ given that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}{a}_{n}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-3)-\\frac{2}{5}(x-3)^{2}+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{n(x-3)^{n}}{n+3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{4}{5}(x-3)+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}.\n$$"}]}
